1
00:00:00,170 --> 00:00:03,440
A mastodon left a trace in northern Greenland.

2
00:00:03,820 --> 00:00:04,550
Not a bone.

3
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:05,700
Not a tusk.

4
00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:06,830
DNA.

5
00:00:07,170 --> 00:00:13,090
Broken pieces of genetic material, recovered from sediment roughly two million years old.

6
00:00:13,420 --> 00:00:16,370
Look at that place today: a polar desert.

7
00:00:16,750 --> 00:00:20,230
Now try to fit an elephant-like animal into the picture.

8
00:00:20,580 --> 00:00:21,980
Something is missing.

9
00:00:22,330 --> 00:00:23,580
Not just the animal.

10
00:00:24,010 --> 00:00:25,380
The world around it.

11
00:00:25,730 --> 00:00:32,880
In 2022, scientists reported evidence of a mastodon lineage from Greenland's Kap Copenhagen Formation.

12
00:00:33,180 --> 00:00:36,580
Plant remains had already hinted at a greener past.

13
00:00:37,080 --> 00:00:40,700
But the dirt held another way into that vanished landscape.

14
00:00:41,040 --> 00:00:43,250
And the giant was only the beginning.

15
00:00:43,650 --> 00:00:46,050
Because finding a trace is one thing.

16
00:00:46,440 --> 00:00:52,820
Working out what left it, how it survived, and what sort of world it belonged to is a very different puzzle.

17
00:00:53,483 --> 00:00:57,383
An animal does not have to die in front of you to leave evidence.

18
00:00:57,793 --> 00:01:00,743
Living things shed biological material.

19
00:01:01,143 --> 00:01:03,703
After death, more breaks down.

20
00:01:04,153 --> 00:01:11,223
Water can carry some of it away, mixing it with material from plants and other animals before it settles into sediment.

21
00:01:11,573 --> 00:01:15,533
Think of it as a delivery system with the address labels torn off.

22
00:01:15,953 --> 00:01:23,503
That is environmental DNA: genetic traces collected from the surroundings, rather than from one intact body.

23
00:01:23,973 --> 00:01:25,963
But delivery creates a problem.

24
00:01:26,373 --> 00:01:29,003
A river can bring material from somewhere else.

25
00:01:29,393 --> 00:01:31,713
A deposit can gather it over time.

26
00:01:31,963 --> 00:01:34,203
So this is not one frozen instant.

27
00:01:34,443 --> 00:01:41,773
And roughly two million years is the age inferred for the deposits, not a date stamped onto each molecule.

28
00:01:42,193 --> 00:01:46,303
Before the pieces can reveal a world, they first have to survive one.

29
00:01:46,973 --> 00:01:49,163
DNA is not built to last forever.

30
00:01:49,483 --> 00:01:50,583
Its strands break.

31
00:01:50,813 --> 00:01:52,643
Its chemical letters suffer damage.

32
00:01:52,823 --> 00:01:56,363
Most of the information can disappear long before anyone comes looking.

33
00:01:57,123 --> 00:02:00,003
Yet cold conditions can slow that loss.

34
00:02:00,343 --> 00:02:05,413
And some DNA can bind to mineral surfaces in the sediment, which may help protect it.

35
00:02:06,073 --> 00:02:08,563
The dirt was not merely covering the clues.

36
00:02:09,283 --> 00:02:11,433
Parts of it may have helped them last.

37
00:02:11,793 --> 00:02:19,123
But protection came with a catch: material that holds on to DNA can also make it difficult to get the DNA back out.

38
00:02:19,353 --> 00:02:22,523
Researchers had to extract and read what remained.

39
00:02:22,843 --> 00:02:25,123
Not an untouched instruction book.

40
00:02:25,563 --> 00:02:29,443
Fragments of one, mixed with fragments from many other lives.

41
00:02:29,843 --> 00:02:32,133
Enough to investigate, perhaps.

42
00:02:32,133 --> 00:02:35,143
But a fragment does not arrive wearing a name tag.

43
00:02:35,453 --> 00:02:38,373
How do you know which animal you are reading?

44
00:02:39,083 --> 00:02:42,543
Compare the surviving sequences with genetic references.

45
00:02:42,913 --> 00:02:43,813
Look for matches.

46
00:02:44,183 --> 00:02:49,503
That sounds simple, until two related animals share similar stretches of DNA.

47
00:02:49,813 --> 00:02:52,553
Or the reference you need is missing.

48
00:02:53,003 --> 00:02:57,003
A match can support a family branch without identifying one exact species.

49
00:02:57,363 --> 00:03:02,903
There is another trap: modern contamination could resemble the signal you hoped to find.

50
00:03:03,223 --> 00:03:06,393
So the question is not just, does this match?

51
00:03:06,713 --> 00:03:09,513
It is, what else could explain it?

52
00:03:09,883 --> 00:03:16,273
Researchers assessed characteristic damage in ancient DNA, alongside other checks and filtering.

53
00:03:16,523 --> 00:03:19,013
Damage was not only something to work around.

54
00:03:19,293 --> 00:03:23,303
It could also help assess whether a signal was genuinely old.

55
00:03:23,763 --> 00:03:27,313
None of that turns a fragment into a complete genome.

56
00:03:27,663 --> 00:03:30,053
It makes the identification more careful.

57
00:03:30,723 --> 00:03:38,033
And when the supported identifications came together, the story grew far beyond a single giant.

58
00:03:38,807 --> 00:03:39,407
Hares.

59
00:03:39,747 --> 00:03:40,357
Reindeer.

60
00:03:40,697 --> 00:03:41,187
Geese.

61
00:03:41,517 --> 00:03:42,147
Rodents.

62
00:03:42,497 --> 00:03:44,537
Evidence of a mastodon lineage.

63
00:03:44,877 --> 00:03:52,267
And around those animal traces, plants: poplar, birch, conifers, shrubs and herbs.

64
00:03:52,767 --> 00:03:56,607
The impressive thing was not simply how large one animal might have been.

65
00:03:56,927 --> 00:03:59,337
It was how much the surroundings had changed.

66
00:04:00,127 --> 00:04:09,117
This combination points to a community very different from today's polar desert, not a neat copy of one familiar modern forest.

67
00:04:09,477 --> 00:04:12,947
And DNA did not discover that greener past alone.

68
00:04:13,217 --> 00:04:17,097
Pollen and visible plant remains had already provided clues.

69
00:04:17,617 --> 00:04:24,037
The genetic evidence added another route, especially into animal life with scarce fossil traces.

70
00:04:24,527 --> 00:04:30,167
Suddenly, the mastodon was no longer a strange detail floating in an empty landscape.

71
00:04:30,447 --> 00:04:33,527
It belonged to a larger ecological picture.

72
00:04:34,097 --> 00:04:38,777
But how much of that picture can we actually bring into focus?

73
00:04:39,507 --> 00:04:41,157
For a moment, imagine it.

74
00:04:41,537 --> 00:04:44,547
An elephant-like animal moving past trees.

75
00:04:44,917 --> 00:04:46,927
A goose lifting from the water.

76
00:04:47,237 --> 00:04:51,557
Plants where today's landscape looks almost empty.

77
00:04:52,087 --> 00:04:56,777
That is our reconstruction, not a recovered afternoon.

78
00:04:57,077 --> 00:05:01,987
The fragments do not tell us that this animal passed that tree while that bird took flight.

79
00:05:02,317 --> 00:05:07,747
Remember the delivery system: water moves material, and deposits gather it over time.

80
00:05:08,077 --> 00:05:12,747
Nor do these traces give us the giant's exact coat, size or behaviour.

81
00:05:13,007 --> 00:05:18,307
The evidence can support a community without supplying every detail of its members.

82
00:05:18,587 --> 00:05:20,287
That boundary matters.

83
00:05:20,537 --> 00:05:27,877
Without it, a plausible scene can quietly become a false memory of something scientists actually saw.

84
00:05:28,317 --> 00:05:31,317
Yet drawing the boundary does not erase the world behind it.

85
00:05:31,657 --> 00:05:36,667
It tells us which parts we have evidence for—and which parts we are imagining.

86
00:05:37,330 --> 00:05:40,800
There is a mistake we can make even when we draw nothing extra.

87
00:05:41,190 --> 00:05:45,860
We can assume that whatever is missing from the DNA was missing from the landscape.

88
00:05:46,130 --> 00:05:50,290
But perhaps an organism's material did not survive.

89
00:05:50,660 --> 00:05:52,980
Perhaps it never reached the samples.

90
00:05:53,340 --> 00:05:56,610
Perhaps the surviving pieces could not be recognised.

91
00:05:56,880 --> 00:05:59,730
No signal is not the same as no animal.

92
00:05:59,930 --> 00:06:02,350
That is why different kinds of evidence matter.

93
00:06:02,680 --> 00:06:08,840
In this study, DNA revealed plant groups that were not recorded there through pollen or larger remains.

94
00:06:09,200 --> 00:06:11,580
Different methods leave different gaps.

95
00:06:11,880 --> 00:06:16,260
Put them together, and the picture can grow without pretending it is complete.

96
00:06:16,580 --> 00:06:20,510
We cannot count every life that passed through this place.

97
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:26,320
We can still discover that the place once held a world very different from the one we see now.

98
00:06:26,920 --> 00:06:28,000
Come back to the dirt.

99
00:06:28,210 --> 00:06:29,870
No tusk curling out of it.

100
00:06:30,100 --> 00:06:31,960
No skeleton waiting underneath.

101
00:06:32,220 --> 00:06:35,860
No complete genome from which to rebuild a mastodon.

102
00:06:36,160 --> 00:06:38,070
Just surviving fragments.

103
00:06:38,420 --> 00:06:45,120
Yet those fragments helped connect an animal to a vanished community, and an almost empty-looking place to a greener past.

104
00:06:45,900 --> 00:06:50,770
At the beginning, the puzzle was how to fit a mastodon into northern Greenland.

105
00:06:51,090 --> 00:06:55,010
The answer was not to squeeze the animal into today's landscape.

106
00:06:55,310 --> 00:06:59,260
It was to discover how different the landscape used to be.

107
00:06:59,660 --> 00:07:01,640
We have not brought that world back.

108
00:07:01,990 --> 00:07:05,410
We have learned to recognise some of what it left behind.

109
00:07:06,160 --> 00:07:07,800
That forest is gone.

110
00:07:08,170 --> 00:07:10,060
Those ancient lives are over.

111
00:07:10,440 --> 00:07:13,820
What looked like empty ground was an incomplete record.

112
00:07:14,180 --> 00:07:15,940
And we had learned to read part of it.

113
00:07:16,270 --> 00:07:18,160
This is Extinct Life.
